00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:01.589 align:start position:0%
 
yeah  when anna and john found out she was  pregnant with elias there was joy hope  but also fear  their first three pregnancies had ended  with miscarriages they couldn't bear to  think of it happening again you don't  expect it to be such a  heart-wrenching thing i guess because in  many ways it's just a very medical  experience you know they don't talk  about it as a baby they talk about it as  a fetus and  you know it's surgery and drugs and  hospitals and there's no kind of  consideration that  to me that that's a tiny human  and that's a tiny human that was going  to come into my life  and now they're gone um and you know  they're never gonna come back  but each time anna felt pressure to get  back to the office the biggest mistakes  that we made with those miscarriages was  um me going back to work  so soon and  it really dragged out the grief for me  so i was grieving for months whereas if  there was a specific leave made for if  you've gone through a miscarriage then  it gives you permission i think to take  it and i think that's really important  it's estimated that as many as one in  four pregnancies ends in miscarriage but  one survey found over a third of  employees didn't know their rights  currently there's no statutory  entitlement to paid leave for people who  lose a baby before 24 weeks of pregnancy  mp angela crawley wants to change that  with a bill to entitle all parents to  paid miscarriage leave i believe it is  for the uk government like  new zealand and like the scottish  government who have introduced three  days paid leave in the public sector and  made that commitment to their public  sector employees that the uk government  could look to do the same my bill would  be primarily to seek dj's paid leave for  parents before 24 weeks and campaigners  want businesses to offer more support  the bottom line is  that you would hope that all employers  want the best for their workforce  but even if they might not have you know  the warm and fuzzy values that that we  wish all employers did  it makes good business sense  in in every way to take care of your  workforce the government says it's  urging employers to show compassion and  flexibility but many feel the rights of  parents don't go far enough  cheers becky johnson sky news derbyshire

